in this video I'm gonna be skipping the  specs and focusing on the real-world  differences after switching from my  beloved iPhone 11 pro max to the s20  ultra for one week and I have to say  that there are two specific reasons that  make me really want to stay using the  Samsung and finally ditch Apple which  I'll talk about in just a bit this was  the first phone that I used the case on  in many years I hate using cases because  it covers up the amazing build quality  and makes them much thicker and heavier  unfortunately even though the s20 ultra  is both taller and thicker than the  11pro max the camera bump is even  thicker and not only does a phone sit  really crooked on a desk but holding in  my hands my finger kept being cut into  by the camera module which doesn't  happen with my iPhone or the note 10  plus it was also pretty much impossible  not to get fingerprints on the camera  while holding horizontally which isn't  an issue with most new phones did I get  used to the large size surprisingly yes  but afterwards going back to the 11pro  max the phone seemed so thin and small  which is something that I thought I  would never say about it next the auto  brightness is stupid maybe it's only  because it's been a week but it's  constantly either too dark or at times  too bright whereas the iPhone gets it  right most of the time I also missed  face ID it just works so well and it's  so fast that most the time I don't even  notice it I mostly used the fingerprint  sensor but I did also try face and lock  for one day of course it isn't as secure  but I wanted to see if it's just as  convenient and it's not you do have more  options but it takes longer to unlock  your phone especially in lower lighting  and at times it doesn't work at all  thankfully the fingerprint sensor has  been much improved since the S 10 and  the note 10 it works fast and pretty  much every time but it's still not as  convenient as face ID I especially  missed it for authentication and apps or  websites where face ID is just automatic  now the screen is gorgeous especially in  1440p mode it is really really nice for  watching movies honestly the best phone  ever and definitely Trump's the iPhone  11 Pro max first off I love having the  1440p option in YouTube that  noticeably more detail than 1080p next  one is HDR this is the first time that  HDR shows a huge significantly  noticeable difference compared to  good-quality  SDR on a phone and it's really  impressive and clearly better than the  iPhone not only that but the speakers  are quite a bit louder as well  [Music]  I'm glad that Samsung has really stepped  up at Spieker game in the last two years  I usually run the iPhone at about 90% or  so but the s20 was just fine at 70% and  in those cases where you have a quiet  video or you're in a loud room and I  want to share a video having the extra  volume is so nice to have the only  downside with the speakers is that you  really feel the case of the phone  vibrate when you're running above 70%  which could be annoying if you're  watching the long video whereas the  iPhone is solid even maxed out but of  course it doesn't get as loud I also  love the split sound adjustments and the  mode that allows notifications to come  through the phone and not the Bluetooth  speaker you're connected to  I really wish my iPhone had these  features along with other Android  features such as picture and picture  YouTube videos chromecast controls and  notifications sticking in the top bar  until I clear them so I don't forget  things now the killer feature of the  display is that 120 Hertz paired with  the fast animations and man do I love it  I especially love it when scrolling  through websites where the lack of  motion blur looks really nice and going  back to my iPhone it just feels so slow  and laggy now there are downsides and  it's not just battery life which of  course is affected if you're using 120  Hertz you have to be in 1080p mode and I  have to say that I did notice it if this  was a five and a half inch screen I may  have not noticed it but being that this  thing is almost 7 inches you can clearly  see it especially in text now the other  downside of course is battery life this  is the first time in a while where I've  had to top off every evening which I've  only had to do on my pro max if I forgot  to charge it the night before I would  have to say that for my usage pattern  and my background apps it's a difference  of about two hours of screen on time in  the 120 Hertz mode and that's using the  default optimized power mode and without  the always-on display if you've never  experienced the freedom of not having to  worry about your battery life that the  iPhone 11 pro max offers then you might  not understand it but it is amazing and  going back to having the top off  throughout the day or an evening is a  bigger deal than I expected once I knew  that I had to top off the phone anyways  I switched to high performance mode and  I turned on the always-on display which  a feature I personally really like and I  wish that my iPhone had even if it would  use extra battery I really wish that  Samsung had the promotion tech of the  iPad pro where it could jump from 120  Hertz down to 60 or even down to 24  Hertz depending on what you're doing  which really helps save battery life  most of our YouTube videos are at 24 fps  and in that case the GPU and the screen  have to put out five times more frames  than necessary needlessly draining that  battery hopefully the iPhone 12 will  have the promotion tech of the iPad and  at full resolution as well and now on to  what I probably love the most and where  I noticed the biggest difference between  my iPhone 11 Pro Max and the s20 ultra  and that is the data speeds and the  reception Samsung has been ahead of  Apple and LTE speeds for a while now  which I loved with the note 10 but the  s20 ultra takes it to a whole new level  now does depend on what provider you use  and where you live but I used t-mobile  and they have the sub 6 5g across the US  this isn't a millimeter wave insanely  fast 5g but one that penetrates  buildings better I often got one or two  bars more with s 20 which is great but  what's even better is the speeds which  were routinely 2 to 3 times faster than  my iPhone during this testing I got my  fastest speed I have ever seen on my  iPhone which was 65 megabits per second  download but in that same scenario the  s20 got 162 and the download speed was  49 instead of 19 the test before that my  iPhone got 25 and the Samsung got about  95 and during peak time with lower  reception the Samsung got 27 megabit per  second while my iPhone got 9 now these  are just speed test numbers so what does  this mean in the real world well a whole  lot actually when I'm using LTE I often  have to limit YouTube videos to 720p or  else I get pauses whereas the Samsung  placed back smoothly at 1440p an initial  buffering of videos is almost  unnoticeable with s20 where I do have to  wait on my iPhone you also notice a  difference when loading websites and if  you're downloading apps and other media  and this is especially noticeable if  you're  constantly in steel or concrete  buildings or basements and ultimately it  could make a difference between having a  signal or not having one at all I really  really really hope that we do have a 5g  option on the upcoming iPhone 12 and if  we don't at least a much better modem  because the difference is huge and for  some people this difference alone is  enough to switch brands another  noticeable difference is call volume and  the s20 ultra is definitely quieter  which because a problem if the person on  the other side isn't very loud  I found myself constantly trying to  press the phone really deep kind of into  my ear and trying to up the volume to  make sure it's maxed out and I think  that might be because this little  speaker is just hidden inside of the  frame here now such on the 